[?2004l[?2004h>         "answer": "Safety risks listed for Lopinavir-Ritonavir include: serious drug interactions (leading to severe, life-threatening, or fatal events); toxicity in preterm neonates due to ethanol and propylene glycol excipients (including cardiac toxicity, lactic acidosis, renal failure, CNS depression, and respiratory complications); pancreatitis (sometimes fatal, associated with marked triglyceride elevations); hepatotoxicity (hepatic dysfunction, elevated transaminases, hepatic decompensation); QT interval prolongation and torsade de pointes; PR interval prolongation (AV block); new onset diabetes mellitus or hyperglycemia; immune reconstitution syndrome; lipid elevations (large increases in total cholesterol and triglycerides); fat redistribution; increased bleeding in patients with hemophilia; and potential for HIV-1 resistance/cross-resistance.",
[?2004l[?2004h>         "citations_raw": [
[?2004lstan[?2004h>             {
[?2004l [?2004h>                 "set_id": "4b1caefa-e6a2-42f3-9194-6dc1dd9e6d85",
[?2004l4b1cae[?2004h>                 "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0014",
[?2004l [?2004h>                 "section": "5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions",
[?2004l[?2004h>                 "target_text": "Initiation of lopinavir and ritonavir, a CYP3A inhibitor, in patients receiving medications metabolized by CYP3A or initiation of medications metabolized by CYP3A in patients already receiving lopinavir and ritonavir, may increase plasma concentrations of medications metabolized by CYP3A."
[?2004l[?2004h>             },
